var e=`# CHAPTER 5 — Mr. Nestor's Shop

First problem of living on the street: where to sleep.

It's not the most urgent — the most urgent is food — but it's the one that scares you the most, because sleep is when you've got no defense. Five years old, on the street, and the night is a beast.

I solved it with Mr. Nestor. He never knew. Or he knew and pretended not to. An old man is sharp, so I prefer to believe he knew.

---

Mr. Nestor had a little shop, odds and ends and repairs. Shoelaces, batteries, a counter for fixing radios, that kind of thing. He closed around seven at night, put the padlock on, and went home.

I found out that the little window on the back storeroom didn't latch right. After he left, I'd climb in there, lie down on the burlap sacks between the boxes, and slip out before dawn. I did that for a good while — not every night, but most of them.

The shop smelled like coffee grounds and old wood. There was a little space heater Mr. Nestor left on the lowest setting, I think for the cat. The cat hated me for a week and then adopted me. It was my first commercial contract: it let me sleep, and I didn't tell anyone it was sleeping inside the shop instead of hunting mice. Confidentiality both ways.

And hold on to this shop. Mr. Nestor and that little store come back in the last chapter, and they come back important.

---

Before the shop, in the first weeks, I ate off what people gave me.

I'd come up, make the face, ask for food. And it worked. People pity a kid on the street. For a couple of weeks, pity is a business model that balances the books.

Then I saw the problem.

---

Pity spoils. Pity spoils like milk out of the fridge.

Week one, you're the kid on the street and everybody wants to help. It's nice, everybody feels good. Week four, you're the kid who's still on the street, and now you're a problem that didn't get solved. The same lady who gave you bread on Monday crosses the street on Friday to avoid you.

And it's not because she's mean. It's that tired pity turns into guilt, and nobody can carry guilt for long. So the person erases you from view, because it's easier.

I was five and I understood that the hard way, watching that lady cross the street. And I thought: *I can't depend on something that goes sour. I need something that turns over.*

---

Money turns over. Pity doesn't turn over, pity goes sour.

That was more or less the conclusion, and it changed everything. I was going to have to earn mine, in a way that repeated, every day, without depending on anybody's mood.

I had no idea how yet. But the decision was made, and you already know what happens when I decide something.

---

The good part of this chapter is a light.

Mr. Nestor left a bulb burning out front of the shop all night. Not for me, obviously. It was to scare off thieves. But that light grazed the back wall, and the storeroom where I slept ended up with, I don't know, one percent of brightness.

You don't know the value of one percent of light until you've slept in zero percent. That one percent was a luxury. I slept practically like a king.

---

I spent two years like that. From five to seven. Sleeping at Mr. Nestor's shop when I could, in some gap when I couldn't, begging at first and getting by after.

And it was at seven that I turned a corner too fast and ran head-on into a kid in a cap who was also turning too fast.

Both of us coming off a fight. Both of us on a hair trigger.

You know how that ended — on the ground, the two of us going at each other over nothing. What you don't know is that the next day I went looking for him again. On purpose.

Because a guy who takes a beating alongside you and comes back the next day is rare. Rarer than honest people. And rare is something I collect.

His name was Kim. And from there on the story stops being just mine.
`;export{e as default};
